i'm joined by neil schmidt who's a  foreign policy expert with the spd with  the social democrats here at the german  parliament now uh mr schmidt what is  your reaction to boris johnson resigning  as british prime minister  first of all this is very good news for  british democracy  after all this opens the opportunity for  the conservative party to return to  serious politics for the uk  and at the same time i hope for a better  relationship between the uk and the  european union in the future  what kind of a new leader does britain  need in order for relations between  germany and the uk to improve  i think we need a fundamental shift in  the attitude of the conservative party  vis-a-vis the european union for too  long the eu has been  considered as the main scapegoat for all  the evil that has been  brought about brought upon  great britain  and i think that a closer eu uk  relationship  can offer  many opportunities  for improving the living standards of  the average britain as well  and when you look at the people who are  currently being discussed as potential  new leaders and are there  hopes that this will improve or does  britain need a complete fresh start  well a fresh start can only be  can only come from the labour party of  course we are hopeful that our sister  party  can win the next general election  but for the time being very much depends  on the personality of the next prime  minister  is it another hard brexiteer  or is it rather a sort of neo-liberal  thatcher  that right that might that is interested  in a rational and sober relationship  with the european union and its uh  single market  when you look at labor your sister party  we're not getting the impression here  that they would change anything about  the pro-brexit stance that also boris  johnson's government stood for  that's right after all brexit  found a large or found a majority at the  referendum so we have to respect that  but i think the british government any  british government  should have a  more  sober  a more  rational  attitude towards the european union and  i think that labor is ready  to get brexit done  without giving up on a working  relationship with the european union  and questions like the future of the  northern ireland protocol  can be solved on that basis in a much  better way than  we could have done with the boris  johnson  just how damaged is the relationship  between the european union  and the uk because of boris johnson's  reign as prime minister  very open uh lee i must say that he has  been regarded as a clown and so  i think we need  some serious and honest politics to  get back to whitehall and downing street  number 10.  and i believe that  a new prime minister  should not regard great britain as an  island some aware offshore in the  indo-pacific  after all great britain is part of  europe and the war in ukraine russia's  aggression against ukraine has  demonstrated to us  how important it is to have a close  security cooperation between the uk and  the european union  now talking about the war in ukraine at  some point did you ever think that  his resignation and the  ensuing um continuous debate that is  going to happen in britain now over the  next couple of weeks about who is going  to follow him that that might actually  weaken the west in its unity when it  comes to reacting to events in ukraine  not at all i'm i have full confidence in  the british government  to be able to act  immediately and forcefully if  national security is under threat and  we've seen a large consensus in the uk  in the british parliament on the war in  ukraine and on the need to support  ukraine uh including uh the  the the shipment of weapon veterans to  ukraine so i see no weakening of the  british position on that  newt schmidt foreign policy expert with  the social democrats here in germany at  the parliament thank you very much  welcome  you
 

